  "notes": "Eurodance built on bluegrass/folk instrumental samples. `confidence` is low because the research rates the entry medium-low — \"the folk-sample premise and era are documented; everything else is genre convention and marked\". The single defining parameter the entry does give is the lead instrument: \"a sampled or replayed fiddle/banjo riff rather than a synth\", so the melody is a busy riff loop at 7-13 notes a bar with a 0.70 riff contour and a fiddle timbre hint, not a sung topline. The harmony follows from the same source — \"major-mode modal (Mixolydian-leaning) vamps inherited from the folk source material — a genuine outlier in a minor-dominant lane\" `[LOW-CONF]`, repeated here — which is why the families run I-bVII-IV and the scales are Mixolydian-weighted. The kit adds \"hand-claps and stomps layered on to imply a barn dance\", encoded as a snap/perc/woodblock lane at 4-8 hits a bar, with \"fills are tambourine 16ths\" rather than snare rolls. Vocals are gang-chanted call-and-response, hence the answering counter. ⚠ Every velocity, density and probability above is INFERRED.",
